1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:07,640
Three days before Hurricane Melissa hit Jamaica, an AI running on my desk drew this line.

2
00:00:07,660 --> 00:00:10,640
It missed the landfall by about fifty kilometres.

3
00:00:10,660 --> 00:00:14,770
So can an AI that saw this coming beat the weather betting market?

4
00:00:21,020 --> 00:00:27,770
This is AIFS, the weather AI from the European forecast centre, ECMWF.

5
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:33,400
The weights are open, so it runs right here, on a desk computer called a DGX Spark.

6
00:00:33,420 --> 00:00:34,760
Here's a real run.

7
00:00:34,780 --> 00:00:39,400
One step of the forecast, six hours of weather, takes about three seconds.

8
00:00:39,420 --> 00:00:43,440
My old mini PC needed almost nine minutes for the same step.

9
00:00:43,460 --> 00:00:47,120
A whole ten-day forecast now takes about three minutes.

10
00:00:47,140 --> 00:00:49,200
First check: Los Angeles.

11
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:52,760
The AI against what the airport station actually measured.

12
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:55,520
Within two degrees Celsius, every time.

13
00:00:55,540 --> 00:00:56,880
And it's not a toy.

14
00:00:56,900 --> 00:01:01,250
ECMWF runs this same model for real, four times a day.

15
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:06,730
Weather AIs made their name on hurricanes, so that's where I started.

16
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:09,530
Three big Atlantic storms from last year.

17
00:01:09,780 --> 00:01:13,290
For each one, I took the AI's forecast from three days before.

18
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:17,610
Melissa: the line runs almost on top of the real track.

19
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:21,770
Imelda, passing Bermuda: about sixty kilometres off.

20
00:01:22,020 --> 00:01:25,760
Erin, off the Outer Banks: about a hundred and twenty.

21
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:27,000
Here's every run.

22
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:29,440
Five days out, it can be way off.

23
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:33,560
One day out, three of the four land within about thirty-five kilometres.

24
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:37,600
Isaias, this year's storm, ran right here on the desk.

25
00:01:37,620 --> 00:01:39,200
So the AI is good.

26
00:01:39,220 --> 00:01:41,850
The question is whether good is worth money.

27
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:44,730
Now the betting part.

28
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:49,330
Kalshi runs a market every day on the high temperature in big US cities.

29
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:51,010
I picked the six busiest.

30
00:01:51,260 --> 00:01:53,050
The bets come in brackets.

31
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:57,730
This was one of my real bets: Phoenix, eighty-five to eighty-six degrees.

32
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:00,210
I bought NO for fifty-four cents.

33
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:03,090
If the high lands anywhere else, it pays a dollar.

34
00:02:03,420 --> 00:02:04,690
It hit eighty-seven.

35
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:05,810
Paid.

36
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:07,250
Who decides the high?

37
00:02:07,580 --> 00:02:11,970
The market's own rules: one airport station, and the data from The Weather Company.

38
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:16,930
I checked every backtest day against the National Weather Service's own climate reports.

39
00:02:17,260 --> 00:02:20,370
Seven hundred eighteen out of seven hundred twenty matched.

40
00:02:20,700 --> 00:02:22,130
And this is going mainstream.

41
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:25,170
Kalshi's odds are coming to the weather app on your phone.

42
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:28,570
There are people selling bots for this.

43
00:02:28,900 --> 00:02:34,410
This one is a weather bot for sixty-seven dollars, built on the free GFS forecast.

44
00:02:34,820 --> 00:02:36,370
But there's a trap.

45
00:02:36,500 --> 00:02:38,650
The market pays on one thermometer.

46
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:44,370
In Los Angeles, it's at the airport, and the AI's nearest point is eight kilometres away.

47
00:02:44,580 --> 00:02:46,080
There's a second trap.

48
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:49,440
The AI only looks at the sky every six hours.

49
00:02:49,460 --> 00:02:52,880
The real peak, mid-afternoon, falls in between.

50
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:54,640
So its high reads cold.

51
00:02:54,660 --> 00:02:57,200
In LA, by six degrees on average.

52
00:02:57,220 --> 00:02:59,610
In New York, almost nothing.

53
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:04,970
Straight out of the box, the raw AI is worse than the free forecast in four of six cities.

54
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:07,650
The fix is simple.

55
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:12,930
For each station, take the AI's average miss over the last thirty days, and add it back.

56
00:03:13,260 --> 00:03:18,090
After the fix, the average miss drops from three and a half degrees to under two.

57
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:19,810
Better than the free forecast.

58
00:03:20,220 --> 00:03:21,930
New York got a little worse.

59
00:03:21,980 --> 00:03:23,570
It was fine to begin with.

60
00:03:24,820 --> 00:03:26,610
So, the backtest.

61
00:03:26,740 --> 00:03:28,810
Sixty days I didn't tune on.

62
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:38,410
Four players, same rules, paper money: the free forecast, the raw AI, the corrected AI with a simple rule, and the corrected AI with a judge.

63
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:40,050
Three of them lose.

64
00:03:40,100 --> 00:03:43,530
The simple rule loses the most: over a hundred dollars.

65
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:44,490
Here's why.

66
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:50,330
Score every forecast against what really happened, and the market's own price beats all of them.

67
00:03:50,420 --> 00:03:52,170
The market already knows.

68
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:53,410
I made it easier.

69
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:54,410
I made it harder.

70
00:03:54,660 --> 00:03:56,890
Higher fees, pickier thresholds.

71
00:03:57,140 --> 00:03:58,730
No version made money.

72
00:03:59,980 --> 00:04:04,370
The fourth player has a judge: a small decision model, running here too.

73
00:04:04,700 --> 00:04:08,130
It reads each bet's numbers and says bet, or skip.

74
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:09,570
Here it says bet.

75
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:12,610
Here it skips a bet the simple rule would take.

76
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:14,370
That one would have lost a dollar.

77
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:16,450
And here, a close call.

78
00:04:16,620 --> 00:04:18,610
Right on the line, so it skips.

79
00:04:18,860 --> 00:04:23,210
Over sixty days, the judge ends up two dollars and ninety-four cents ahead.

80
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:25,960
The simple rule: down a hundred and six.

81
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:27,450
That's about break-even.

82
00:04:27,500 --> 00:04:29,010
Not proof of an edge.

83
00:04:30,260 --> 00:04:32,490
And here's the part I almost missed.

84
00:04:32,580 --> 00:04:34,650
I nearly went live with a bug.

85
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:38,810
The first version fit a straight line for each station, on summer days.

86
00:04:39,140 --> 00:04:43,890
In October, Phoenix's forecast was colder than anything in that summer cloud.

87
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:47,690
The line just kept going, and said eighty-five and a half degrees.

88
00:04:48,100 --> 00:04:50,960
The National Weather Service said seventy-nine.

89
00:04:50,980 --> 00:04:53,640
I caught it in a dry run, with no money.

90
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:59,560
I locked new rules, version two, and posted their fingerprints on GitHub before any real bet.

91
00:04:59,580 --> 00:05:03,880
That bug would have bet on a six-cent long shot, calling it a huge edge.

92
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:06,480
The real high was seventy-seven.

93
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:10,720
On paper, version one of the live player lost ninety-three dollars.

94
00:05:10,740 --> 00:05:13,280
Version two made two dollars ninety-four.

95
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:16,320
But I designed version two after seeing those days.

96
00:05:16,340 --> 00:05:19,130
So the only honest test left was real money.

97
00:05:20,380 --> 00:05:24,130
Then, ten dollars of real money, for one locked week.

98
00:05:24,460 --> 00:05:27,690
On days one and two, the bot placed four bets.

99
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:31,130
Every pick was posted on GitHub before its market settled.

100
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:32,210
Two won.

101
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:33,410
Two lost.

102
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:36,210
Then, on day three, it crashed.

103
00:05:36,300 --> 00:05:41,970
Kalshi started reporting one settled temperature as a range instead of a number, and my bot choked on it.

104
00:05:42,220 --> 00:05:44,610
Every morning, for the rest of the week.

105
00:05:44,700 --> 00:05:47,010
So only two of the seven days traded.

106
00:05:47,100 --> 00:05:49,970
No order outside the week, nothing left unfilled.

107
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:52,930
The result: minus fifty cents.

108
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:54,610
Here's the account itself.

109
00:06:01,020 --> 00:06:05,730
So, can an AI that saw a hurricane coming beat the weather betting market?

110
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:06,920
Not yet.

111
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:08,480
The forecast is great.

112
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:13,080
But the market's price is better, because it already knows what the AI knows.

113
00:06:13,100 --> 00:06:17,960
My best player was about break-even on paper, and down fifty cents with real money.

114
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:19,640
Three things you can use.

115
00:06:19,660 --> 00:06:24,320
One: a great forecast isn't an edge if the market already has it.

116
00:06:24,340 --> 00:06:27,560
Two: check where the market measures, and when.

117
00:06:27,580 --> 00:06:30,920
Three: test your bot on days you didn't tune it on.

118
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:34,840
Everything is open: the code, the rules, every pick.

119
00:06:34,860 --> 00:06:37,650
Would you have trusted that eighty-five and a half degrees?
